items.completion:
A C program can be terminated in several ways. Here are a few common methods:
1. Return statement: The main function can return to the operating system, which will terminate the program. For example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    return 0;
}
```
2. Exit function: The exit function can be used to terminate a program. For example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    exit(0);
    return 0;
}
```
3. Abort function: The abort function can be used to terminate a program. For example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    abort();
    return 0;
}
```
4. Raise an exception: An exception can be raised using the raise function. For example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    raise(SIGABRT);
    return 0;
}
```
5. Signal: A signal can be sent to a program using the kill function. For example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    kill(getpid(), SIGABRT);
    return 0;
}
```
Note that the above methods will terminate the program immediately. If you want to terminate the program after a certain amount of time, you can use the sleep function. For example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    sleep(1);
    return 0;
}
```
